A1. A unit of fat. ~ POUND
A6. A bull fighter. ~ DEBUNKER
A13. More fitting than fit. ~ APTER
A14. You need a good grip on this when you have to face the music. ~ BATON
A15. The first lady of the land. ~ EVE
A16. A thin kind of piping. ~ REEDY
A17. Where people think bill-collectors should go. ~ AWAY
A18. To exchange false teeth for an old master. ~ SWOP
A19. Forward! ~ ON
A20. Over the river. ~ SPAN
A22. This is on tap every season in Vermont. ~ MAPLE
A23. An indoor airship, as the Britons have it. ~ LIFT
A26. A recent national freeze-up. ~ ASSET
A29. Gatti-Casazza heavyweight. ~ DIVA
A30. Pep. ~ ENERGY
A32. A famous French Jane. ~ ARC
A34. A snake in fish's clothing. ~ EEL
A35. Football profit. ~ GAIN
A36. Two birds that will net one girl one stone ~ RIVALS
A39. Sign of nobility. ~ DE
A41. This is de trop. ~ TOO
A42. Leading sky pilot. ~ ACE
A43. Instrument for moving water vessels. ~ OAR
A45. Dora thought Wash., D. C. had direct current after finding out about these initials. ~ AC
A47. The colored lights of Harlem. ~ SMOKES
A49. Follows cold, soft & ginger. ~ SNAP
A51. When M. Chevalier does things right. ~ NOW
A53. The most ubiquitous type of before. ~ ERE
A54. Pets. ~ PEEVES
A56. Darn. ~ DRAT
A58. He juggles to get this balance. ~ ADDER
A61. Cull. ~ REAP
A62. What the Spanish lover said to make his girl cry. ~ ADIOS
A63. What the New Yorker very decidedly wasn't when he dumped his garbage into the Grand Canyon. ~ AWED
A65. The biggest part of NRA. ~ NR
A66. The old Green River. ~ NILE
A67. This is hollow. ~ FAME
A69. Broadway show—to state a bare fact. ~ REVUE
